From: MastaG <mastag@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: defer BC-250 OD commits past a PCON modeset without blocking

On a DP-out board driving an active DP->HDMI 2.1 PCON (e.g. a Chrontel
CH7218), the PCON trains its HDMI side autonomously and asynchronously.
DC never drives or waits on that training, so there is no "training in
progress" signal to hook the way native HDMI FRL provides one -- and a
forced SMU clock/voltage commit landing in that window desyncs the link,
which is the black-screen-at-boot this and the companion FRL patch fix.

The first version of this patch polled the PCON's own DPCD status bits
(DP_PCON_HDMI_TX_LINK_STATUS / DP_PCON_HDMI_POST_FRL_STATUS) from the
modeset path for up to 10s, waiting for them to report ready. They never
do. With amdgpu.cs_od_defer_debug=1 on a BC-250 into an LG G5 through a
UGREEN 8K adapter:

  [    9.626492] cs_od_defer: DP-HDMI21 PCON FRL wait started
  [   20.030431] cs_od_defer: DP-HDMI21 PCON FRL wait finished after 10000ms (ready=0)
  [   29.867259] cs_od_defer: DP-HDMI21 PCON FRL wait started
  [   40.270402] cs_od_defer: DP-HDMI21 PCON FRL wait finished after 10000ms (ready=0)

Two invocations per boot, each running the full timeout, neither ever
observing readiness. That is ~20s of added boot time, the same again on
shutdown, and -- worse -- a 10s window per modeset in which a second
display commit can land on a link the first is still bringing up. Both
were reported from the field as "boots and shuts down noticeably slower"
and as screen corruption that a kernel without these patches did not
show.

Arm a deadline and return immediately instead. The commit path defers
while jiffies is before it, which is all the wait was ever there to
achieve; the polling contributed nothing on real hardware. A plain
unsigned long rather than a timer or delayed work, so nothing needs
cancelling when amdgpu unbinds and no reference to the link outlives the
call. amdgpu.cs_pcon_frl_defer_ms tunes the window (default 2000ms, 0
disables the deferral).

Note the initialiser: jiffies starts 300s before its wrap point, so
time_before(jiffies, 0) is true for the first five minutes of uptime.
Initialising the deadline to 0 would refuse every OD commit in that
window; INITIAL_JIFFIES is the value jiffies itself starts at.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_dpms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_dpms.c
@@ -59,6 +59,9 @@
 #include "atomfirmware.h"
 #include "vpg.h"
 
+#include <linux/jiffies.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
+
 #define DC_LOGGER \
 	dc_logger
 #define DC_LOGGER_INIT(logger) \
@@ -68,6 +71,75 @@
 	DC_LOG_HW_HOTPLUG(  \
 		__VA_ARGS__)
 
+/*
+ * Shared with drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/protocols/link_hdmi_frl.c
+ * and drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c -- see the
+ * comment in link_hdmi_frl.c for why Cyan Skillfish's OD clock/voltage
+ * commit path needs to defer around active link training.
+ */
+extern bool cs_od_defer_debug;
+
+/*
+ * Deadline, in jiffies, until which cyan_skillfish_od_edit_dpm_table() keeps
+ * deferring OD commits for a PCON that has just been through a modeset.
+ *
+ * Initialised to INITIAL_JIFFIES -- the value jiffies itself starts at --
+ * rather than to 0. jiffies deliberately starts 300s before its wrap point,
+ * so for the first five minutes of uptime it reads as negative when taken as
+ * a signed long, and time_before(jiffies, 0) would be true for all of it:
+ * every OD commit in the first five minutes after boot would be refused.
+ */
+unsigned long cs_pcon_frl_defer_until = INITIAL_JIFFIES;
+
+uint cs_pcon_frl_defer_ms = 2000;
+module_param(cs_pcon_frl_defer_ms, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(cs_pcon_frl_defer_ms,
+		 "BC-250: how long to defer GPU clock/voltage commits after a modeset through a DP to HDMI 2.1 PCON, in ms (0 = do not defer)");
+
+/*
+ * On a DP-out board driving an active DP->HDMI2.1 PCON (e.g. a Chrontel
+ * CH7218), the PCON trains HDMI FRL to the actual display autonomously and
+ * asynchronously -- DC only ever reads its status via DPCD
+ * (DP_PCON_HDMI_TX_LINK_STATUS / DP_PCON_HDMI_POST_FRL_STATUS), it never
+ * drives or waits on that training itself (see
+ * read_and_intersect_post_frl_lt_status() in link_dp_capability.c, which
+ * checks it once during capability detection, before real video --
+ * and thus before the PCON could have started training -- is even
+ * flowing). There is no existing "training in progress" signal to hook for
+ * this path the way link_hdmi_frl.c's native-HDMI-FRL functions provide.
+ *
+ * This used to poll those two DPCD status bits from here for up to 10s,
+ * blocking the modeset until the PCON reported ready. On real hardware it
+ * never does: with amdgpu.cs_od_defer_debug=1 on a BC-250 into an LG G5
+ * through a UGREEN 8K adapter, every invocation ran the full timeout and
+ * returned ready=0 -- two invocations per boot, 10000ms each. That bought
+ * nothing and cost a great deal: ~20s of added boot time (and the same again
+ * on shutdown), plus a 10s window per modeset during which a second display
+ * commit could land on a link the first was still bringing up.
+ *
+ * So do not wait at all. Arm a deadline and return immediately; the commit
+ * path defers while jiffies is before it, which is the only thing the wait
+ * was ever there to achieve. A plain unsigned long rather than a timer or a
+ * delayed work: nothing has to be cancelled when amdgpu unbinds, and no
+ * reference to this link outlives the call.
+ */
+static void cs_defer_od_for_dp_hdmi21_pcon(struct dc_link *link)
+{
+	if (!link->dc->caps.dp_hdmi21_pcon_support ||
+	    !link->dpcd_caps.dongle_caps.dp_hdmi_regulated_autonomous_mode_support)
+		return;
+
+	if (!cs_pcon_frl_defer_ms)
+		return;
+
+	WRITE_ONCE(cs_pcon_frl_defer_until,
+		   jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(cs_pcon_frl_defer_ms));
+
+	if (cs_od_defer_debug)
+		pr_info("amdgpu: cs_od_defer: DP-HDMI21 PCON modeset, deferring OD clock/voltage commits for %ums\n",
+			cs_pcon_frl_defer_ms);
+}
+
 #define RETIMER_REDRIVER_INFO(...) \
 	DC_LOG_RETIMER_REDRIVER(  \
 		__VA_ARGS__)
@@ -2697,6 +2769,8 @@
 	dc->hwss.unblank_stream(pipe_ctx,
 		&pipe_ctx->stream->link->cur_link_settings);
 
+	cs_defer_od_for_dp_hdmi21_pcon(link);
+
 	if (stream->sink_patches.delay_ignore_msa > 0)
 		msleep(stream->sink_patches.delay_ignore_msa);
 
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/pm/swsmu/smu11/cyan_skillfish_ppt.c
@@ -142,6 +142,14 @@
 extern bool cs_od_defer_debug;
 
 /*
+ * Set by drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc/link/link_dpms.c to a deadline a
+ * short way into the future when a modeset goes out through an active
+ * DP->HDMI 2.1 PCON, which trains its HDMI side autonomously with no status
+ * DC can wait on. See the comment there.
+ */
+extern unsigned long cs_pcon_frl_defer_until;
+
+/*
  * On an 8-core board, decode the metrics table the way the pre-patch SMU
  * firmware laid it out. Only needed on an older BIOS that unlocks the two
  * extra cores without carrying the 8-core metrics firmware patch; with that
@@ -957,6 +965,13 @@
 			return -EBUSY;
 		}
 
+		if (time_before(jiffies, READ_ONCE(cs_pcon_frl_defer_until))) {
+			if (cs_od_defer_debug)
+				dev_info(smu->adev->dev,
+					 "cs_od_defer: deferring OD commit, DP-HDMI21 PCON is still bringing its link up\n");
+			return -EBUSY;
+		}
+
 		if (cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk < CYAN_SKILLFISH_SCLK_MIN ||
 		    cyan_skillfish_user_settings.sclk > CYAN_SKILLFISH_SCLK_MAX) {
 			dev_err(smu->adev->dev, "Invalid sclk! Valid sclk range: %uMHz - %uMhz\n",
